WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.260
"They hacked in through email." "The attacker moved around the network." None of that is precise enough to compare incidents or build detections.

2
00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:15.880
MITRE ATT&CK fixes that — a free, global knowledge base of real adversary behavior.

3
00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:20.100
Think of it like a standardized medical coding system.

4
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:34.330
Just as ICD-10 lets any two hospitals describe the same condition identically, ATT&CK lets any two analysts, anywhere, describe the same attacker behavior with the exact same identifier — no matter which vendor's alert raised it.

5
00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:44.600
ATT&CK comes in three matrices. Enterprise — Windows, macOS, Linux, cloud, network, containers — the one a SOC analyst lives in.

6
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.480
Mobile — Android and iOS behavior.

7
00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:52.400
And ICS — industrial control systems, the operational-technology world.

8
00:00:52.900 --> 00:01:01.560
Here's the Enterprise matrix. Each column is a tactic — the attacker's goal at a moment in time. There are fourteen of them in all.

9
00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:09.530
Initial Access, Execution, Persistence, Privilege Escalation, Defense Evasion, Credential Access, Command and Control, and more.

10
00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:14.350
Inside each tactic sit the techniques — the specific ways to achieve that goal.

11
00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:26.400
Each one has an ID. T1566 is Phishing, a technique under Initial Access — and sub-techniques go one level deeper, like T1566.001 for a malicious attachment.

12
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:33.430
So remember the split: a tactic is the why, a technique is the how, and a sub-technique is the exact how.

13
00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:39.530
Learn to speak in ATT&CK IDs, and the whole industry understands you instantly.
